From 2fe2c5e9cf2f9e15edea357d86746073d978c486 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Lakr <git@qaq.wiki>
Date: Mon, 9 Nov 2026 04:06:33 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 03/24] Time device lock holds per holder, and how long a vCPU
 waited

A vCPU that publishes on the IOSurface mapper ring blocks on the device
lock for as long as the drain worker holds it, and a vCPU stopped in an
MMIO exit answers no interrupt, including the kernel's cross-call to stop
every CPU for its debugger; a guest panic "DebuggerXCallEnter ... synch
pending on cpu N" is what that looks like. vcpu_lock_wait counts the
waits; nothing named the holder that made them long.

Every acquisition of the device lock now goes through a timed guard that
names its holder: the drain tranche, device reset, the vCPU's mapper
register reads and writes, the vCPU's direct GFX register reads and
writes, the console scanout copy, the poll, the action pop, and the
display readers. Each records its hold, after releasing the lock, into a
log2 histogram per holder, and the vCPU's contended waits go into another.
Once per ten seconds, if any hold or wait in that interval reached 50 ms,
device_lock_hold logs count/max/p99 per holder and the vCPU wait max/p99;
device_lock_hold_boot logs the boot's totals at reset or destroy.

Cost: two clock reads and four relaxed atomic adds per acquisition.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
(cherry picked from commit 6c199b79278e9b80f33ea6d67a90eced44d82f20)
